Yes — **full Unicode, internationalization, text shaping, font handling, and complex-script rendering should be part of the architecture**, not an afterthought.

For the later prompts, we should explicitly include a dedicated text stack:

```text
RoboUnicode
├── Unicode properties
├── UTF-8 / UTF-16 / UTF-32
├── normalization
├── case mapping
├── grapheme clusters
├── word/line segmentation
├── bidi
├── locale data
└── Unicode version management

RoboI18N
├── locale
├── language/region
├── number formatting
├── date/time formatting
├── plural rules
├── collation
├── messages
└── localization resources

RoboFont
├── font discovery
├── font loading
├── font collections
├── glyph mapping
├── variation fonts
├── fallback
├── emoji fonts
└── font metrics

RoboText
├── script detection
├── bidi processing
├── shaping
├── ligatures
├── kerning
├── glyph runs
├── line breaking
├── text measurement
└── vertical writing where appropriate

RoboTextRender
├── glyph rasterization
├── antialiasing
├── hinting
├── subpixel/advanced rendering where supported
└── integration with RoboPaint/RoboGraphics
```

And this needs to feed into:

```text
RoboHTML
   ↓
RoboDOM
   ↓
RoboCSS
   ↓
RoboLayout
   ↓
RoboText
   ↓
RoboFont
   ↓
RoboTextRender
   ↓
RoboPaint
   ↓
RoboCompositor
   ↓
RoboGraphics
```

We should also explicitly support scripts such as **Arabic, Urdu, Persian, Hindi, Bengali, Chinese, Japanese, Korean, Thai, Hebrew, Devanagari**, etc., including right-to-left text and complex shaping.

One important architectural distinction: we can use Chromium as a **reference for understanding the required behavior**, but we should not blindly copy Chromium's implementation. For Unicode/font standards, we'll also need to account for the relevant Unicode specifications and font/text standards and use appropriately licensed third-party libraries/data where implementing everything ourselves would be unreasonable.

So I'll treat **full international text support as a first-class requirement** in the subsequent prompts, especially when we reach RoboString, RoboUnicode, RoboFont, RoboText, RoboLayout, and RoboRender.

Yes. Here is the **full roadmap** so you can see where Roboticela Runtime is going from the current Unicode work all the way to a complete independent browser/runtime stack.

The exact goal numbers may be adjusted as implementation reveals dependencies, but this is the intended roadmap.

